{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part5.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part5.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Immense Dividend Hike Puts Dividend Growth Stocks in the Spotlight\",\n   \"publisher\": \"morningstar.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Is Nvidia a True Dividend Growth Stock?\\nWhat’s the Big Deal About Dividend Growth Anyway?\\nWhy Are Dividend Growth Stocks Performing Well in 2026?\\nWhat Does Nvidia Joining the Dividend Growth Party Mean?\\nNvidia’s Immense Dividend Hike Puts Dividend Growth Stocks in the Spotlight\\nCompanies growing their dividends have posted strong returns this year. Why is that?\\nLost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement. In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\\nAnalysts will be quick to point out that perspective is key. “Nvidia’s dividend is virtually immaterial relative to its financial health and forward prospects,” wrote Brian Colello , who covers semiconductor stocks for Morningstar. Nvidia’s dividend yield will continue to register well below 1%. And even more of the company’s cash is being spent buying back shares.\\nBut in absolute terms, Nvidia’s dividend is huge. Something like $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders. That sum will make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US stock market.\\nThe Nvidia news also comes at a time when the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index is neck and neck with the broad US stock market, which is unusual in an up year. So, what’s going on with dividend growth stocks this year? And what does Nvidia’s dividend hike suggest, if anything?\\nDespite the dividend increase, it’s probably too soon to call Nvidia a dividend growth stock. Although the company initiated a quarterly dividend back in 2013, the payout was paltry until the 2026 increase. The Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index requires five consecutive years of dividend growth. Other dividend indexes on the market require an even longer history. Active managers running dividend growth strategies interpret their mandates in different ways.\\n“Most of Nvidia’s distributions to shareholders come in the form of share repurchases,” writes Colello. That’s consistent with the overall US stock market trend. As I wrote about recently , companies have spent far more buying back their own shares than they have on dividends.\\nIndeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration. The company’s preference when it comes to cash allocations is clear in the exhibit below.\\nSource: Nvidia 10-K and 10-Q statements reconciled with Morningstar Indexes data by analyst Aryan Singh. Data as of July 26, 2026. Trailing 12-month data used. Buybacks depicted on a gross basis. Download CSV .\\nDividend growth has long been viewed as a symbol of corporate “quality.” Only profitable companies secure in their financial positions consistently increase cash returned to shareholders. While share buybacks can be opportunistic, dividends are a commitment. The market typically punishes dividend cutters.\\nThe nearly 400 stocks in the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index include a mix of market leaders and less familiar names. Mega-caps like Microsoft MSFT , JPMorgan Chase JPM , Johnson & Johnson JNJ , and ExxonMobil XOM , which sit atop the index because constituents are weighted by market capitalization, are joined by mid- and small-cap stocks like Becton Dickinson BDX , DTE Energy DTE , and American Financial Group AFG .\\nInvestment style-wise, the dividend growth index leans toward value. Technology stocks, which tend to carry higher-than-average multiples, represent just 17.6% of index weight. That’s less than half their share of the broad US stock market. Meanwhile, the generally lower-priced financial services, healthcare, industrials, and consumer defensive sectors are overrepresented among dividend growers.\\nWhat about performance? Dividend growers have a reputation for being defensive stocks. Strong balance sheets and moderate price tags mean that dividend growers often shine during risk-off market environments.\\nIndeed, the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index has generally outperformed during selloffs. It declined by less than the broad US stock market in down years 2018 and 2022. It also held its value relatively well during the tariff turmoil of March-April 2025 and in negative months like March and June 2026. From a volatility perspective, the dividend growth index has registered a standard deviation of returns well below the market.\\nGiven its defensive profile, it’s somewhat surprising that the dividend growth index is performing well in 2026. After all, the market is up double digits as the fourth quarter approaches. The technology sector has led this year, powered by the AI buildout. Those conditions aren’t typically favorable to dividend growers.\\nThe energy sector helps explain dividend growth’s strong returns. With the conflict in the Middle East pushing up oil prices, energy companies are booking huge profits. Key index constituents include ExxonMobil, with a near four-decade-long history of dividend growth, and ConocoPhillips COP , which rebuilt its dividend growth track record after low oil prices forced a dividend cut in 2016.\\nBut the biggest contributor to 2026 performance—both in absolute and relative terms—is the healthcare sector. Johnson & Johnson, Merck MRK , and UnitedHealth UNH are all having stellar years. J&J is a paragon of dividend growth going back to the 1960s. Merck and UnitedHealth have more modest histories in the neighborhood of 15 years, but UnitedHealth has posted double-digit dividend growth rates.\\nThe strong year for dividend growth reflects a number of trends. First, market leadership broadened from the narrow group of Magnificent Seven companies that dominated 2023-25. Nvidia, for example, has performed well, but not at the same level as in recent years. Amazon.com AMZN , Alphabet GOOGL , and Meta Platforms META have lagged. Second, this has been a year in which value stocks have outperformed growth. That includes the energy and healthcare sectors, but also value technology stocks benefiting from investments. Third, it has generally been a strong year for companies returning cash to shareholders, as I wrote about recently .\\nActually, this isn’t the first time dividend growers have outperformed in an up market. The dividend growth index also posted stellar returns in 2021, another year of market “breadth.” All US sectors posted strong returns then, and little separated growth and value stocks.\\nSome might see Nvidia’s dividend hike as a sign the company is entering a new, more slow-growth life stage. That doesn’t fit with the 70% revenue growth forecast. One thing that’s certain is that Nvidia’s cash pile is massive.\\nNvidia is also part of a trend I’ve written about regarding tech and tech-adjacent companies paying more dividends. Microsoft and Apple AAPL have both been constituents of the dividend growth index for years. In 2024, Meta and Alphabet both initiated payouts to shareholders.\\nNvidia also fits the dividend growth profile when it comes to profitability and financial strength. Increasing a payout can be seen as a sign of corporate quality. Because of their defensive characteristics, dividend growers are a good option for investors concerned about a market bubble .\\nMorningstar, Inc., licenses indexes to financial institutions as the tracking indexes for investable products, such as exchange-traded funds, sponsored by the financial institution. The license fee for such use is paid by the sponsoring financial institution based mainly on the total assets of the investable product. A list of ETFs that track a Morningstar index is available via the Capabilities section at indexes.morningstar.com. A list of other investable products linked to a Morningstar index is available upon request. Morningstar, Inc., does not market, sell, or make any representations regarding the advisability of investing in any investable product that tracks a Morningstar index.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"about $25 billion a year returned through the dividend\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the 2,400% increase of the dividend\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 3,\n     \"text\": \"the $0.25 quarterly dividend\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"title\": \"Dear Nvidia Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for September 24\",\n   \"publisher\": \"barchart.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor technology.\\nHuang's presence is particularly notable for Nvidia, given the company's position at the center of the global AI chip market and the ongoing U.S.-China technology relationship. Investors will be watching whether discussions around AI and semiconductor trade create any signals relevant to Nvidia's access to the Chinese market, export restrictions, or the broader outlook for AI infrastructure demand.\\nIntel vs. TSMC: One Chip Stock Controls 72.5% of the Foundry Market. The Other Is Mounting a Comeback.\\nWhile the dinner itself does not guarantee any policy changes, Sept. 24 could provide an important backdrop for Nvidia stock as the market weighs the potential implications of renewed U.S.-China engagement.\\nNvidia is a global leader in accelerated computing and AI, renowned for pioneering the GPU that revolutionized gaming, data centers, and AI-driven computing. Headquartered in Santa Clara, California, Nvidia's technology now powers everything from high-performance gaming and cloud computing to autonomous vehicles and generative AI applications. With a market cap of $5.5 trillion, Nvidia stands among the world's most valuable companies, driven by its dominance in AI infrastructure and continued innovation in next-generation chip design.\\nNvidia stock has gained steady momentum in recent sessions, with the shares now sitting just 4% below their 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. The stock is up 22% year-to-date (YTD) and 28% over the past 52 weeks, while its five-day gain stands at 7%, reflecting an acceleration in recent trading. Although in the red this morning, Nvidia has otherwise advanced in each of the past five sessions through Sept. 22, including gains of 2.5% on Sept. 17, 1.3% on Sept. 18, 2.3% on Sept. 21, and 0.66% yesterday, Sept. 22. It surged marginally in the last session, closing at $228.87.\\nNvidia's recent stock advance has been supported by several developments reinforcing the strength of the AI infrastructure cycle. The company's latest quarterly results showed revenue growth of 106%, while management has continued to signal strong demand for its next-generation AI systems.\\nMeanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong. The stock is also benefiting from renewed optimism around the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin platforms as customers continue expanding AI infrastructure.\\nIn addition, expectations surrounding the upcoming Trump-Xi engagement have added another potential catalyst, with investors watching for any developments that could improve Nvidia's ability to sell AI chips in China.\\nNVDA trades at 24.04 times forward earnings and 24.81 times sales, which is currently a premium compared to industry peers.\\nNvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\nThe company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\\nNvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\\nFurthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\\nIt's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\\nBernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon reiterated a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on NVDA stock this week while maintaining a $400 price target. The target was unchanged from Rasgon's earlier revision, when Bernstein raised it from $315 to $400 on Aug. 27, underscoring the analyst's continued confidence in Nvidia's earnings and AI-driven growth outlook.\\nFurthermore, Rothschild & Co Redburn analyst Timm Schulze-Melander maintained a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on Nvidia with a $325 price target.\\nOverall, NVDA stock has a consensus \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating. Of the 50 analysts covering it, 46 advise a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" three suggest a \\\"Moderate Buy,\\\" and one offers a \\\"Strong Sell\\\" rating.\\nThe average analyst price target for NVDA is $325.88, indicating a potential upside of 45%. The Street-high target price of $515 suggests that the stock could rally as much as 129%.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"title\": \"What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"text\": \"What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\\nTexas Instruments (TXN) and NVIDIA both sell chips for the data centers now being built. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA’s revenue, while TI sells to industrial and automotive customers as well as data centers. You pay 38.2 times the yearly profit for TI, yet only 28.1 times for NVIDIA. Their latest earnings calls show the two businesses parting ways on supply.\\nNVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\nTI guided Q3 2026 revenue to $5.65 billion to $6.15 billion, against $5.5 billion in Q2. TI’s data center sales doubled from a year earlier in Q2. Management said most of the Q3 growth will come from selling more units, not from higher prices. TI also has clean room space it can equip if demand keeps rising.\\nThe two outlooks are different kinds of news. Demand for NVIDIA’s chips is larger than NVIDIA’s supply, so supply sets the pace. TI has spare room to make more, so demand sets TI’s pace.\\nEach company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\\nNVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\\nTI’s cost is its factories. TI spent $3.3 billion on capital projects in the year to Q2 2026. TI’s $8.7 billion of cash from operations covered that more than twice over. TI’s free cash flow over the same period included $1.6 billion of CHIPS Act incentives. The yearly numbers on growth, margin, and debt show how far apart the two already are.\\nThis comparison scores six standard measures of price, growth, profit and debt , and NVIDIA leads Texas Instruments on all six. NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\\nDebt equals 0.7% of NVIDIA’s market value, against 6.1% for TI. NVIDIA carries almost no debt against its value.\\nTI wins on one measure outside those six: price to sales. You pay 11.9 times the yearly revenue for TI, against 17.9 times for NVIDIA. The six scored measures judge price on profit, and on profit NVIDIA is cheaper.\\nTI also has a long dividend record. In September, TI raised the quarterly dividend to $1.52 a share, from $1.42. That was TI’s 23rd straight year of dividend increases.\\nWhat Would Count As A Stumble For NVIDIA?\\nNVIDIA’s lead is wide enough that it would take a real stumble to shrink it. Revenue below NVIDIA’s own guided range for fiscal Q3 would be the first sign of one. NVIDIA holds its call on that quarter on November 17. A result inside or above the range would leave NVIDIA’s lead in place.\\nNVIDIA leads on growth, margins, debt, and price on profit. TI’s strengths are a lower price on sales, spare capacity, and a long dividend record. Revenue below NVIDIA’s guided range in November is the first result that would test that lead.\\nHow To Act On TXN?\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\\n\\\"Now, compute is revenue,\\\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\\nWhen might it happen?\\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the fiscal second quarter's 75% gross margin\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"gross margin of 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n     \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"title\": \"China May Let Alibaba Buy New Nvidia Chips, The Information Says\",\n   \"publisher\": \"bloomberg.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"text\": \"(Bloomberg) -- The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter.\\nEllison Pledges $9.2 Billion More in Oracle Shares for Loans\\nMcDonald's Selloff Hits 30% as Big Mac Inflation Spurs Pushback\\nThe Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was released this month, including how many of them and what they would use the product for, the report said, adding that the ministry told some of the firms it intends to approve the purchases.\\nNvidia has faced export restrictions from the US as well as curbs from Beijing over the sale of its chips to China, due to a web of bilateral tensions ranging from security to competition on artificial intelligence.\\nThe US administration recently lifted restrictions on some Nvidia chips, though the products tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold in small quantities.\\nGLP-1 Users Are Furious After Employers Drop Insurance Benefits\\nThis $2 Billion Company Dresses HBO's The Pitt Cast and Real Doctors Too\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": null\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"title\": \"Why Nvidia (NVDA) Stock Is Trading Up Today\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"text\": \"Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 2.7% in the morning session after the company authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion.\\nAccording to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028. CEO Jensen Huang said in a statement that Nvidia's growth is being driven by a \\\"once-in-a-generation platform shift to AI and accelerated computing,\\\" and that strong cash generation lets the company invest in that buildout while returning capital to shareholders. Speaking on CNBC's \\\"Squawk Box,\\\" Huang called the AI infrastructure cycle the largest buildout in human history and said Nvidia plans to return more cash to shareholders as it generates it. A record buyback authorization typically supports the stock by signaling management confidence and reducing share count when cash flow is strong.\\nAfter the initial pop, the shares cooled down to $231.14, up 2.7% from the previous close.\\nNvidia's shares are not very volatile and have only had 8 moves greater than 5% over the last year. In that context, today's move indicates the market considers this news meaningful, although it might not be something that would fundamentally change its perception of the business.\\nThe biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\\nAccording to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\\nNvidia is up 22.4% since the beginning of the year, and at $231.14 per share, it is trading close to its 52-week high of $235.74 from May 2026. Investors who bought $1,000 worth of Nvidia's shares 5 years ago would now be looking at an investment worth $11,167.\\nALSO WORTH WATCHING: Nvidia's Quiet Partner. Nvidia's chips cost a hundred grand. The connectors that make them work cost even more. One company makes them all.\\nEvery AI server needs specialized infrastructure the chip companies don't make. High-speed cables. Power connectors. Thermal sensors. This 90-year-old company built a monopoly on it. The AI boom just started. This stock is still flying under the radar. Claim The Stock Ticker Here for FREE .\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the $92.37 billion revenue estimate for the quarter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the $96.22 billion revenue of the quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n     \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n     \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ],\n \"answers\": [\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B41\",\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported second-quarter revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year-over-year, and adjusted earnings per share of $2.22; both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates of $92.37 billion in revenue and $2.09 in adjusted earnings per share, according to the company's press release and earnings commentary.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"According to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Quarter, up 106% year-over-year\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year-over-year\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Year-over-year\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Adjusted\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue consensus estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"$92.37 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted earnings per share consensus estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.09\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia projected 70% revenue growth for its next fiscal year (fiscal 2028), according to the article's account of the company's second-quarter report, which it dates to about one month before the 28 September 2026 article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B42\",\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results for the quarter ended July 26, 2026, on Aug. 26, 2026: revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year from $46.7 billion and 18% sequentially, exceeding prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%; non-GAAP net income was $54 billion, up 118% from $24.8 billion; adjusted EPS was $2.22, up 120% and above expectations; non-GAAP gross margin was 75% versus 72.5%; operating income was $63.7 billion, up 124% year over year; and Data Center segment revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year over year and the majority of total company revenue.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n      \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\",\n      \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"record; up 106% year over year from $46.7 billion and 18% sequentially; exceeded prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Prior revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$91 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; actual revenue exceeded it\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$54 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up 118% from $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; up 120%; exceeding expectations\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"versus 72.5% in Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"Operating income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$63.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up 124% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 7,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up 117% year over year; majority of total company revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points; the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"GAAP and non-GAAP; plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The guidance explicitly assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China, setting the baseline for the China market-access question: any resumption of China data-center revenue would be upside to this outlook.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Street expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% year over year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Street expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analysts/Street consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$9.25\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst consensus; growth of 102.4% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"Analysts/Street consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.33\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst consensus; growth of 65.7% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"Analysts/Street consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B43\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (quarter ended 26 July 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier and faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth, with $89 billion of the total from the data center business and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, as reported by fool.com's Daniel Sparks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up over a year earlier\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal first quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"year-over-year growth\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center business share of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"net income growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"126%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"rose year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, about 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, an outlook she called \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\"; fool.com characterised the constraint as manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028 (the year ending in January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"described by the CFO as “a supply-constrained outlook”\",\n       \"party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management describes the fiscal 2028 revenue outlook as supply-constrained rather than demand-constrained, a premise bearing on how large NVIDIA's purchase and supply commitments to secure capacity will be at the next annual report; the item gives no commitment amount.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes take effect, a small drop from the 75% gross margin reported for the fiscal second quarter, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"gross margins should bottom at this level\",\n       \"party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"settle at this level as price hikes kick in\",\n       \"party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts' consensus already has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion, according to fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"analysts' consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B44\",\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results (quarter ended July 26, 2026, reported Aug. 26): revenue of a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year from $46.7 billion and up 18% sequentially, exceeding prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%; non-GAAP net income of $54 billion, up 118% from $24.8 billion; adjusted EPS of $2.22, up 120%; non-GAAP gross margin of 75% versus 72.5%; operating income of $63.7 billion, up 124% year over year; and Data Center segment revenue of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and the majority of total revenue.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n      \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\",\n      \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"record; GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"$46.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth sequentially\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"18%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$54 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"$24.8 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 7,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"adjusted (non-GAAP)\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 8,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; prior-year 72.5%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 9,\n       \"measure\": \"Operating income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$63.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 10,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"segment; up 117% YoY\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points; management said the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"GAAP and non-GAAP, plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's Q3 FY2027 forecast explicitly assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China, establishing the baseline that any restored China chip access would change; it does not indicate whether U.S. or Chinese approval will be given.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-7\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts' consensus expects NVIDIA EPS growth of 102.4% year over year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by a further 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Street expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Sell-side analysts (consensus)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$9.25\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"102.4%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.33\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"65.7%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B45\",\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B46\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion within $96 billion of total revenue in fiscal Q2 2027, and management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a midpoint the article calculates as about 12% above the Q2 total.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"midpoint about 12% above the fiscal Q2 total\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and reset its margin expectations: it guided gross margin (sales left after production costs) to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2, and expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Each company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected to bottom\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — a portion of the business resting on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"invested in AI labs\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"share of NVIDIA's business from NVIDIA-backed AI labs\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States the scale of NVIDIA's equity investments in AI labs (nearly $50 billion) and management's expectation that NVIDIA-funded AI labs provide roughly a quarter of its fiscal 2028 business — directly relevant to the thesis's comparison of disclosed guarantees/credit support against equity investments in AI labs and cloud partners.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B47\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA told shareholders in May 2026 that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase the article's author calculates at 2,400%; the author estimates roughly $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders through the dividend.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\",\n      \"But in absolute terms, Nvidia’s dividend is huge. Something like $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"prior quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$0.01 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"annual dividend payout\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion per year\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"author's approximation\",\n       \"party\": \"Morningstar (article author)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Indeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share buyback program\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$80 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has forecast 70% year-on-year revenue growth, cited by the article as one of the company's news items in 2026; the item gives no period for the forecast.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"year-on-year revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article cites, among NVIDIA's news this year, its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments; it does not say how much of that is credit support or guarantees rather than equity investment.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"artificial intelligence investments financed with NVIDIA's involvement\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$500 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Morningstar\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Indicates the scale of NVIDIA's financing role in AI investments, the activity whose disclosed guarantees and credit support clean-1 tracks; the item does not break out guarantees or revenue backstops from equity investment, so it speaks only to magnitude.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B48\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total revenue, as cited by Trefis in its comparison with Texas Instruments.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"total company\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a midpoint about 12% above the fiscal Q2 2027 total.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; midpoint about 12% above the fiscal Q2 2027 total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal Q3 2027 gross margin to 74%, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin, share of sales left after production costs\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin guidance, down from fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance (expected trough)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company expectation that gross margin bottoms in fiscal Q4 2027\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, according to Trefis.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"cumulative investment in AI labs as of the article; no date given\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives the scale of NVIDIA's equity investment in AI labs, the figure clean-1's outcome contrasts with the credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees NVIDIA discloses at its next annual report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028, a part of the business that would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of business from NVIDIA-backed AI labs\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"management expectation; share of NVIDIA's business, not a revenue amount\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's expectation of how much of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 business comes from NVIDIA-backed AI labs bears directly on the exposure the guarantees, credit support and equity investments tracked by clean-1 create.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e8\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's revenue grew 83% over the past year and the company kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, per Trefis's comparison with Texas Instruments.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"past year\",\n       \"value\": \"83%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company revenue growth over the past year as computed by Trefis\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"operating margin\",\n       \"period\": \"past year\",\n       \"value\": \"65.2 cents of each sales dollar\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"operating profit as a share of sales, company\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B49\",\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, raising the total authorization to $235 billion, and said it expects to complete the remaining buyback through fiscal 2028; the company described the increase as the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 2.7% in the morning session after the company authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion.\",\n      \"According to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"additional share buyback authorization\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$150 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total share buyback authorization\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$235 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year-over-year, and adjusted earnings per share of $2.22, according to a company press release and earnings commentary; both topped Wall Street consensus estimates of $92.37 billion revenue and $2.09 adjusted EPS.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\",\n      \"According to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year-over-year\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"adjusted earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"consensus revenue estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$92.37 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"consensus adjusted earnings per share estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.09\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia projected 70% revenue growth for the next year, fiscal 2028, in the second-quarter results announcement reported about a month before this article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"next year (fiscal 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B50\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total revenue, as summarised by Trefis from the company's latest earnings call. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA's revenue.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"total company\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which Trefis calculates is about 12% above the fiscal Q2 total.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said memory prices rose more than it expected and that it reset its margin expectations: gross margin was guided to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in fiscal Q2, and is expected to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations.\",\n      \"NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin expectation (bottom)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin, expected trough\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — business that would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\",\n      \"Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investments in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"invested in AI labs\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"share of NVIDIA's business from NVIDIA-backed AI labs\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company business, AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives the size of NVIDIA's equity investments in AI labs (nearly $50 billion), the figure the thesis's outcome compares against disclosed guarantees and credit support, and quantifies management's expectation that NVIDIA-funded AI labs reach roughly a quarter of its fiscal 2028 business.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part5.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"articles\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"title\\\"],a.get(\\\"publication_date\\\")); print(\\\"TEXT\\\",a[\\\"text\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] not in [2,4,7] else a[\\\"text\\\"][-3500:] if a[\\\"article\\\"]==7 else \\\"seen\\\"); print(\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\")); print(\\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"]);\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  print(\\\"STATEMENT\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_party\\\"),\\\"DATE\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_date\\\"),\\\"FIGURES\\\",[(f[\\\"n\\\"],f[\\\"measure\\\"],f[\\\"value\\\"],f[\\\"kind\\\"],f.get(\\\"party\\\")) for f in s[\\\"figures\\\"]],\\\"LINKS\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\"))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part5.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"articles\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"title\\\"],a.get(\\\"publication_date\\\")); print(\\\"TEXT\\\",a[\\\"text\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] not in [2,4,7] else a[\\\"text\\\"][-3500:] if a[\\\"article\\\"]==7 else \\\"seen\\\"); print(\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\")); print(\\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"]);\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  print(\\\"STATEMENT\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_party\\\"),\\\"DATE\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_date\\\"),\\\"FIGURES\\\",[(f[\\\"n\\\"],f[\\\"measure\\\"],f[\\\"value\\\"],f[\\\"kind\\\"],f.get(\\\"party\\\")) for f in s[\\\"figures\\\"]],\\\"LINKS\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\"))'\"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 2 Nvidia’s Immense Dividend Hike Puts Dividend Growth Stocks in the Spotlight 2026-09-23\nTEXT seen\nNAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'about $25 billion a year returned through the dividend'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'the 2,400% increase of the dividend'}, {'figure': 3, 'text': 'the $0.25 quarterly dividend'}]\nQUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}]\nARTICLE 4 Dear Nvidia Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for September 24 2026-09-23\nTEXT seen\nNAMED []\nQUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}]\nARTICLE 7 What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not? 2026-09-25\nTEXT fer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\nTexas Instruments (TXN) and NVIDIA both sell chips for the data centers now being built. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA’s revenue, while TI sells to industrial and automotive customers as well as data centers. You pay 38.2 times the yearly profit for TI, yet only 28.1 times for NVIDIA. Their latest earnings calls show the two businesses parting ways on supply.\nNVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\nTI guided Q3 2026 revenue to $5.65 billion to $6.15 billion, against $5.5 billion in Q2. TI’s data center sales doubled from a year earlier in Q2. Management said most of the Q3 growth will come from selling more units, not from higher prices. TI also has clean room space it can equip if demand keeps rising.\nThe two outlooks are different kinds of news. Demand for NVIDIA’s chips is larger than NVIDIA’s supply, so supply sets the pace. TI has spare room to make more, so demand sets TI’s pace.\nEach company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\nNVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\nTI’s cost is its factories. TI spent $3.3 billion on capital projects in the year to Q2 2026. TI’s $8.7 billion of cash from operations covered that more than twice over. TI’s free cash flow over the same period included $1.6 billion of CHIPS Act incentives. The yearly numbers on growth, margin, and debt show how far apart the two already are.\nThis comparison scores six standard measures of price, growth, profit and debt , and NVIDIA leads Texas Instruments on all six. NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\nDebt equals 0.7% of NVIDIA’s market value, against 6.1% for TI. NVIDIA carries almost no debt against its value.\nTI wins on one measure outside those six: price to sales. You pay 11.9 times the yearly revenue for TI, against 17.9 times for NVIDIA. The six scored measures judge price on profit, and on profit NVIDIA is cheaper.\nTI also has a long dividend record. In September, TI raised the quarterly dividend to $1.52 a share, from $1.42. That was TI’s 23rd straight year of dividend increases.\nWhat Would Count As A Stumble For NVIDIA?\nNVIDIA’s lead is wide enough that it would take a real stumble to shrink it. Revenue below NVIDIA’s own guided range for fiscal Q3 would be the first sign of one. NVIDIA holds its call on that quarter on November 17. A result inside or above the range would leave NVIDIA’s lead in place.\nNVIDIA leads on growth, margins, debt, and price on profit. TI’s strengths are a lower price on sales, spare capacity, and a long dividend record. Revenue below NVIDIA’s guided range in November is the first result that would test that lead.\nHow To Act On TXN?\nNAMED []\nQUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}]\nARTICLE 8 Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion 2026-09-25\nTEXT Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\n\"Now, compute is revenue,\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\nWhen might it happen?\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\nNAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': \"the fiscal second quarter's 75% gross margin\"}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'gross margin of 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028'}]\nQUESTIONS Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 7, in <module>\n[{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {'id': 'clean-6', 'question': 'Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?', 'period': \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}]\nARTICLE 9 China May Let Alibaba Buy New Nvidia Chips, The Information Says 2026-09-27\nTEXT (Bloomberg) -- The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter.\nEllison Pledges $9.2 Billion More in Oracle Shares for Loans\nMcDonald's Selloff Hits 30% as Big Mac Inflation Spurs Pushback\nThe Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was released this month, including how many of them and what they would use the product for, the report said, adding that the ministry told some of the firms it intends to approve the purchases.\nNvidia has faced export restrictions from the US as well as curbs from Beijing over the sale of its chips to China, due to a web of bilateral tensions ranging from security to competition on artificial intelligence.\nThe US administration recently lifted restrictions on some Nvidia chips, though the products tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold in small quantities.\nGLP-1 Users Are Furious After Employers Drop Insurance Benefits\nThis $2 Billion Company Dresses HBO's The Pitt Cast and Real Doctors Too\nNAMED []\nQUESTIONS None\nARTICLE 10 Why Nvidia (NVDA) Stock Is Trading Up Today 2026-09-28\nTEXT Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 2.7% in the morning session after the company authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion.\nAccording to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028. CEO Jensen Huang said in a statement that Nvidia's growth is being driven by a \"once-in-a-generation platform shift to AI and accelerated computing,\" and that strong cash generation lets the company invest in that buildout while returning capital to shareholders. Speaking on CNBC's \"Squawk Box,\" Huang called the AI infrastructure cycle the largest buildout in human history and said Nvidia plans to return more cash to shareholders as it generates it. A record buyback authorization typically supports the stock by signaling management confidence and reducing share count when cash flow is strong.\nAfter the initial pop, the shares cooled down to $231.14, up 2.7% from the previous close.\nNvidia's shares are not very volatile and have only had 8 moves greater than 5% over the last year. In that context, today's move indicates the market considers this news meaningful, although it might not be something that would fundamentally change its perception of the business.\nThe biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\nAccording to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\nNvidia is up 22.4% since the beginning of the year, and at $231.14 per share, it is trading close to its 52-week high of $235.74 from May 2026. Investors who bought $1,000 worth of Nvidia's shares 5 years ago would now be looking at an investment worth $11,167.\nALSO WORTH WATCHING: Nvidia's Quiet Partner. Nvidia's chips cost a hundred grand. The connectors that make them work cost even more. One company makes them all.\nEvery AI server needs specialized infrastructure the chip companies don't make. High-speed cables. Power connectors. Thermal sensors. This 90-year-old company built a monopoly on it. The AI boom just started. This stock is still flying under the radar. Claim The Stock Ticker Here for FREE .\nNAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'the $92.37 billion revenue estimate for the quarter'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'the $96.22 billion revenue of the quarter'}]\nQUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {'id': 'clean-6', 'question': 'Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?', 'period': \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}, {'id': 'clean-7', 'question': \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\", 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.'}]\nANSWER B41 ARTICLE 10\nSTATEMENT ev-2 Nvidia reported second-quarter revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year-over-year, and adjusted earnings per share of $2.22; both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates of $92.37 billion in revenue and $2.09 in adjusted earnings per share, according to the company's press release and earnings commentary. PARTY Nvidia DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'Revenue', '$96.22 billion', 'actual', 'Nvidia'), (2, 'Revenue growth year-over-year', '106%', 'actual', 'Nvidia'), (3, 'Adjusted earnings per share', '$2.22', 'actual', 'Nvidia'), (4, 'Revenue consensus estimate', '$92.37 billion', 'estimate', 'Nvidia'), (5, 'Adjusted earnings per share consensus estimate', '$2.09', 'estimate', 'Nvidia')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT ev-3 Nvidia projected 70% revenue growth for its next fiscal year (fiscal 2028), according to the article's account of the company's second-quarter report, which it dates to about one month before the 28 September 2026 article. PARTY Nvidia DATE about 1 month ago FIGURES [(1, 'Revenue growth', '70%', 'guidance', 'Nvidia')] LINKS none\nANSWER B42 ARTICLE 4\nSTATEMENT ev-1 NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results for the quarter ended July 26, 2026, on Aug. 26, 2026: revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year from $46.7 billion and 18% sequentially, exceeding prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%; non-GAAP net income was $54 billion, up 118% from $24.8 billion; adjusted EPS was $2.22, up 120% and above expectations; non-GAAP gross margin was 75% versus 72.5%; operating income was $63.7 billion, up 124% year over year; and Data Center segment revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year over year and the majority of total company revenue. PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 FIGURES [(1, 'Revenue', '$96.2 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'Prior revenue guidance', '$91 billion', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'Non-GAAP net income', '$54 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (4, 'Adjusted EPS', '$2.22', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (5, 'Non-GAAP gross margin', '75%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (6, 'Operating income', '$63.7 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (7, 'Data Center revenue', '$89 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT ev-2 NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points; the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 FIGURES [(1, 'Revenue guidance', '$108 billion', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'Gross margin guidance', 'approximately 74%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS [{'question': 'clean-3', 'new_question': False, 'reason': 'The guidance explicitly assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China, setting the baseline for the China market-access question: any resumption of China data-center revenue would be upside to this outlook.'}]\nSTATEMENT ev-5 The Street expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% year over year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028. PARTY Analysts/Street consensus DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'EPS', '$9.25', 'estimate', 'Analysts/Street consensus'), (2, 'EPS', '$15.33', 'estimate', 'Analysts/Street consensus')] LINKS none\nANSWER B43 ARTICLE 8\nSTATEMENT e1 NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (quarter ended 26 July 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier and faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth, with $89 billion of the total from the data center business and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, as reported by fool.com's Daniel Sparks. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'revenue', '$96.2 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'revenue growth year over year', '106%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'revenue growth year over year', '85%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (4, 'data center revenue', '$89 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (5, 'net income', '$59.7 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (6, 'net income growth year over year', '126%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e2 NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, about 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue, as reported by fool.com. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'revenue guidance', '$108 billion', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e3 On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, an outlook she called \"a supply-constrained outlook\"; fool.com characterised the constraint as manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand. PARTY Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO) DATE late August 2026 FIGURES [(1, 'revenue growth guidance', 'roughly 70%', 'guidance', 'Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)')] LINKS [{'question': 'clean-2', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Management describes the fiscal 2028 revenue outlook as supply-constrained rather than demand-constrained, a premise bearing on how large NVIDIA's purchase and supply commitments to secure capacity will be at the next annual report; the item gives no commitment amount.\"}]\nSTATEMENT e4 On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes take effect, a small drop from the 75% gross margin reported for the fiscal second quarter, as reported by fool.com. PARTY Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO) DATE late August 2026 FIGURES [(1, 'gross margin guidance', 'between 71% and 72%', 'guidance', 'Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)'), (2, 'gross margin guidance', '72% to 73%', 'guidance', 'Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)'), (3, 'gross margin', '75%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e5 Analysts' consensus already has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion, according to fool.com. PARTY analysts' consensus DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'revenue', '$412 billion', 'estimate', 'consensus')] LINKS none\nANSWER B44 ARTICLE 4\nSTATEMENT ev-4 NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results (quarter ended July 26, 2026, reported Aug. 26): revenue of a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year from $46.7 billion and up 18% sequentially, exceeding prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%; non-GAAP net income of $54 billion, up 118% from $24.8 billion; adjusted EPS of $2.22, up 120%; non-GAAP gross margin of 75% versus 72.5%; operating income of $63.7 billion, up 124% year over year; and Data Center segment revenue of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and the majority of total revenue. PARTY NVIDIA DATE 2026-08-26 FIGURES [(1, 'Revenue', '$96.2 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'Revenue growth year over year', '106%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'Revenue', '$46.7 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (4, 'Revenue growth sequentially', '18%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (5, 'Non-GAAP net income', '$54 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (6, 'Non-GAAP net income', '$24.8 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (7, 'Adjusted EPS', '$2.22', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (8, 'Non-GAAP gross margin', '75%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (9, 'Operating income', '$63.7 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (10, 'Data Center segment revenue', '$89 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT ev-5 NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points; management said the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'Revenue guidance', '$108 billion', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'Gross margin guidance', '74%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS [{'question': 'clean-3', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Management's Q3 FY2027 forecast explicitly assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China, establishing the baseline that any restored China chip access would change; it does not indicate whether U.S. or Chinese approval will be given.\"}]\nSTATEMENT ev-7 Analysts' consensus expects NVIDIA EPS growth of 102.4% year over year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by a further 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028. PARTY Sell-side analysts (consensus) DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'EPS', '$9.25', 'estimate', 'consensus'), (2, 'EPS growth year over year', '102.4%', 'estimate', 'consensus'), (3, 'EPS', '$15.33', 'estimate', 'consensus'), (4, 'EPS growth year over year', '65.7%', 'estimate', 'consensus')] LINKS none\nANSWER B45 ARTICLE 9\nTypeError: string indices must be integers\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part5.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"][4:]:\n print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"]);\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  if isinstance(s,str): print(\\\"EMPTY\\\",s); continue\n  print(\\\"STATEMENT\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_party\\\"),\\\"DATE\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_date\\\"),\\\"FIGURES\\\",[(f[\\\"n\\\"],f[\\\"measure\\\"],f[\\\"value\\\"],f[\\\"kind\\\"],f.get(\\\"party\\\")) for f in s[\\\"figures\\\"]],\\\"LINKS\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\"))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part5.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"][4:]:\n print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"]);\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  if isinstance(s,str): print(\\\"EMPTY\\\",s); continue\n  print(\\\"STATEMENT\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_party\\\"),\\\"DATE\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_date\\\"),\\\"FIGURES\\\",[(f[\\\"n\\\"],f[\\\"measure\\\"],f[\\\"value\\\"],f[\\\"kind\\\"],f.get(\\\"party\\\")) for f in s[\\\"figures\\\"]],\\\"LINKS\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\"))'\"","aggregated_output":"ANSWER B45 ARTICLE 9\nEMPTY n\nEMPTY o\nEMPTY n\nEMPTY e\nEMPTY  \nEMPTY k\nEMPTY e\nEMPTY p\nEMPTY t\nANSWER B46 ARTICLE 7\nSTATEMENT e1 NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion within $96 billion of total revenue in fiscal Q2 2027, and management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a midpoint the article calculates as about 12% above the Q2 total. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'data center revenue', '$89 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'total revenue', '$96 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'revenue guidance', '$108 billion, plus or minus 2%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e2 NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and reset its margin expectations: it guided gross margin (sales left after production costs) to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2, and expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'gross margin', '74%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'gross margin', '75%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'gross margin', '71% to 72%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e3 NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — a portion of the business resting on customers NVIDIA helps fund. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'investment in AI labs', 'nearly $50 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, \"share of NVIDIA's business from NVIDIA-backed AI labs\", 'roughly a quarter', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"States the scale of NVIDIA's equity investments in AI labs (nearly $50 billion) and management's expectation that NVIDIA-funded AI labs provide roughly a quarter of its fiscal 2028 business — directly relevant to the thesis's comparison of disclosed guarantees/credit support against equity investments in AI labs and cloud partners.\"}]\nANSWER B47 ARTICLE 2\nSTATEMENT e1 NVIDIA told shareholders in May 2026 that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase the article's author calculates at 2,400%; the author estimates roughly $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders through the dividend. PARTY NVIDIA DATE May 2026 FIGURES [(1, 'quarterly dividend per share', '$0.25 per share', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'prior quarterly dividend per share', '$0.01 per share', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'annual dividend payout', '$25 billion per year', 'estimate', 'Morningstar (article author)')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e2 NVIDIA announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration. PARTY NVIDIA DATE May 2026 FIGURES [(1, 'share buyback program', '$80 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e3 NVIDIA has forecast 70% year-on-year revenue growth, cited by the article as one of the company's news items in 2026; the item gives no period for the forecast. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'year-on-year revenue growth', '70%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e4 The article cites, among NVIDIA's news this year, its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments; it does not say how much of that is credit support or guarantees rather than equity investment. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, \"artificial intelligence investments financed with NVIDIA's involvement\", '$500 billion', 'actual', 'Morningstar')] LINKS [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Indicates the scale of NVIDIA's financing role in AI investments, the activity whose disclosed guarantees and credit support clean-1 tracks; the item does not break out guarantees or revenue backstops from equity investment, so it speaks only to magnitude.\"}]\nANSWER B48 ARTICLE 7\nSTATEMENT e1 NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total revenue, as cited by Trefis in its comparison with Texas Instruments. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'data center revenue', '$89 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'total revenue', '$96 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e2 NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a midpoint about 12% above the fiscal Q2 2027 total. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'revenue guidance', '$108 billion', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e5 NVIDIA guided fiscal Q3 2027 gross margin to 74%, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'gross margin', '75%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'gross margin guidance', '74%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'gross margin guidance (expected trough)', '71% to 72%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e6 NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, according to Trefis. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'investment in AI labs', 'nearly $50 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Gives the scale of NVIDIA's equity investment in AI labs, the figure clean-1's outcome contrasts with the credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees NVIDIA discloses at its next annual report.\"}]\nSTATEMENT e7 NVIDIA management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028, a part of the business that would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'share of business from NVIDIA-backed AI labs', 'roughly a quarter', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Management's expectation of how much of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 business comes from NVIDIA-backed AI labs bears directly on the exposure the guarantees, credit support and equity investments tracked by clean-1 create.\"}]\nSTATEMENT e8 NVIDIA's revenue grew 83% over the past year and the company kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, per Trefis's comparison with Texas Instruments. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'revenue growth', '83%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'operating margin', '65.2 cents of each sales dollar', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nANSWER B49 ARTICLE 10\nSTATEMENT e1 Nvidia authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, raising the total authorization to $235 billion, and said it expects to complete the remaining buyback through fiscal 2028; the company described the increase as the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history. PARTY Nvidia DATE 2026-09-28 FIGURES [(1, 'additional share buyback authorization', '$150 billion', 'actual', 'Nvidia'), (2, 'total share buyback authorization', '$235 billion', 'actual', 'Nvidia')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e2 Nvidia reported second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year-over-year, and adjusted earnings per share of $2.22, according to a company press release and earnings commentary; both topped Wall Street consensus estimates of $92.37 billion revenue and $2.09 adjusted EPS. PARTY Nvidia DATE about 1 month ago FIGURES [(1, 'revenue', '$96.22 billion', 'actual', 'Nvidia'), (2, 'revenue growth year-over-year', '106%', 'actual', 'Nvidia'), (3, 'adjusted earnings per share', '$2.22', 'actual', 'Nvidia'), (4, 'consensus revenue estimate', '$92.37 billion', 'estimate', 'Wall Street consensus'), (5, 'consensus adjusted earnings per share estimate', '$2.09', 'estimate', 'Wall Street consensus')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e3 Nvidia projected 70% revenue growth for the next year, fiscal 2028, in the second-quarter results announcement reported about a month before this article. PARTY Nvidia DATE about 1 month ago FIGURES [(1, 'revenue growth', '70%', 'guidance', 'Nvidia')] LINKS none\nANSWER B50 ARTICLE 7\nSTATEMENT e1 NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total revenue, as summarised by Trefis from the company's latest earnings call. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA's revenue. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'data center revenue', '$89 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'total revenue', '$96 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e2 NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which Trefis calculates is about 12% above the fiscal Q2 total. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'revenue guidance', '$108 billion', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e4 NVIDIA management said memory prices rose more than it expected and that it reset its margin expectations: gross margin was guided to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in fiscal Q2, and is expected to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'gross margin guidance', '74%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'gross margin', '75%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'gross margin expectation (bottom)', '71% to 72%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e5 NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — business that would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund. PARTY NVIDIA DATE None FIGURES [(1, 'investments in AI labs', 'nearly $50 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, \"share of NVIDIA's business from NVIDIA-backed AI labs\", 'roughly a quarter', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINKS [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Gives the size of NVIDIA's equity investments in AI labs (nearly $50 billion), the figure the thesis's outcome compares against disclosed guarantees and credit support, and quantifies management's expectation that NVIDIA-funded AI labs reach roughly a quarter of its fiscal 2028 business.\"}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
